WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.280
Thanks to his starring role in two Suicide Squad&nbsp;
movies, Captain Boomerang is a household name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.320
among countless comic fans. But how did&nbsp;
this DC baddie go from obscure rogue to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.920
one of the most recognizable antiheroes in the DC&nbsp;
universe? This is the story of Captain Boomerang.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:22.240
So: Who is Captain Boomerang? The short answer is&nbsp;
that he's George "Digger" Harkness, a shameless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:27.600
crook who will do just about anything to make a&nbsp;
buck. He doesn't boast any supernatural abilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:32.560
instead carrying an arsenal of boomerangs that&nbsp;
he wields with lethal skill. But Boomer's weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:37.600
aren't just run-of-the-mill boomerangs, with&nbsp;
many of them serving specific purposes in combat.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.480
While he acts as kind of a lone&nbsp;
wolf in his earliest appearances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.720
Boomerang quickly becomes more of a team player&nbsp;
— even though he's never very good at getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.040
along with whatever team he's in. He's joined&nbsp;
forces with other prominent villains of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:53.760
fictional Central City over the years, and&nbsp;
more famously, is one of the villains often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.480
pressed into service for the United States&nbsp;
government by the tyrannical Amanda Waller.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.800
Digger Harkness isn't the most patient of men,&nbsp;
either. He has a short temper and can turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:08.080
on his allies with little provocation. Sadly,&nbsp;
he's also demonstrated some deeply problematic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:12.960
beliefs during his many comic book appearances.&nbsp;
Particularly in the early Suicide Squad comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:18.320
Captain Boomerang regularly makes racist and&nbsp;
sexist comments about his teammates. A native&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:23.040
Australian, Digger sees himself as a patriot to&nbsp;
his homeland, though it was later revealed that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.120
because of his criminal exploits, most of&nbsp;
his countrymen are actually ashamed of him.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:33.520
Captain Boomerang first locks horns with Central&nbsp;
City's guardian speedster, the Flash, in 1960's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:38.720
The Flash #117. While the rogue's blue, white,&nbsp;
and black costume wouldn't change much between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:44.000
his debut and his more prominent appearances in&nbsp;
the '80s, the character has changed in other ways.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:50.080
In Flash #117, Digger Harkness gets the idea to&nbsp;
become Captain Boomerang after the W.W. Wiggins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:55.280
Game Company starts searching for a mascot who can&nbsp;
promote their toy boomerangs. Harkness is already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:59.520
a thief at that point, and he uses his unique&nbsp;
boomerang talents to help him in his battles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:04.560
with the Scarlet Speedster. He's initially able to&nbsp;
defeat Flash with one of his lightning boomerangs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.000
and then ties the unconscious Flash&nbsp;
to a giant boomerang and shoots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.480
it into the ocean. Obviously, Flash escapes&nbsp;
and puts an end to the crook's plans.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.760
In these early appearances, Captain Boomerang is&nbsp;
just as goofy as he is later on. Curiously enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:22.800
however, it's never specified in this era that&nbsp;
Digger Harkness is from Australia. The country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.960
isn't mentioned at all, and his dialogue&nbsp;
isn't written to reflect his nationality.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:30.400
What do you do once you've created&nbsp;
enough villains designed to bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:33.520
down a superhero? You throw them&nbsp;
all together in a team, of course!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:37.200
Flash's villainous team-up is known as&nbsp;
the Rogues, who have since gone on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.120
become one of the most enduring groups&nbsp;
of villains in the history of DC Comics.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:44.240
Captain Boomerang is one of the&nbsp;
founding members of the Rogues.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:50.160
He's there in 1962's The Flash #130 to join&nbsp;
forces with contemporaries like Trickster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:55.760
Captain Cold, the Top, and Mirror Master, all&nbsp;
set against their common enemy. Over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:59.600
more villains would join, and just about any&nbsp;
villain who spent enough time taking their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:04.400
lumps fighting the Flash found their way onto the&nbsp;
team. In some cases, the Rogues have stepped into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:08.720
more heroic territory, too. Every now and then,&nbsp;
the group stands against other villains whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:13.680
actions against Central City jump across the kind&nbsp;
of moral lines that even the Rogues won't cross.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:17.760
While Captain Boomerang is one of the first&nbsp;
bad guys to count himself among the Rogues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.600
eventually he becomes less and less&nbsp;
associated with the group — as his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.320
destiny brings him to a very&nbsp;
different team of villains.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:29.920
Nowadays, Captain Boomerang is most famous as&nbsp;
a regular fixture in the modern line-up of Task&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:35.040
Force X, also known as the Suicide Squad. He's a&nbsp;
group member in their very first battle against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:40.880
Brimstone in 1987's Legends #3 and appears&nbsp;
regularly in many of the subsequent volumes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:46.080
of Suicide Squad. He's also on the team in the&nbsp;
Justice League Unlimited episode "Task Force X,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:52.960
as well as in 2014's Batman: Assault on Arkham,&nbsp;
2018's Suicide Squad: Hell to Pay, and 2020's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:57.680
Justice League Dark: Apokolips War. He'll also&nbsp;
be a member of the group in the upcoming video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:02.560
game Suicide Squad: Kill the Justice League,&nbsp;
featured on the team in CW's Arrowverse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:08.160
and of course, was played by Jai Courtney in&nbsp;
2016's Suicide Squad and its 2021 follow-up.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:10.680
"Hey, looky here. Ooh, it's fire. Wooooooh!"

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:17.120
Ironically, in spite of how often he works&nbsp;
with Task Force X, Captain Boomerang is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:21.680
of the most disliked members of the team.&nbsp;
He also makes sure to be a constant thorn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:27.200
in the side of the Squad liaison Amanda Waller,&nbsp;
who frequently refers to him as "Boomerbutt."

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:31.600
It's surprising how often Captain&nbsp;
Boomerang finds himself working for Waller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:35.840
considering what a pain he can be to everyone&nbsp;
involved. On the one hand, you could say it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:40.560
a testimony to his effectiveness in battle&nbsp;
that his presence continues to be tolerated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.760
There does, however, appear to be&nbsp;
a very fine line between the two.

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:48.960
If you had to be in the Suicide Squad, Captain&nbsp;
Boomerang is just about the last guy you'd want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:53.520
to team up with. While the character's portrayal&nbsp;
often changes depending on who's writing him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:58.640
one thing that tends to remain consistent is that&nbsp;
he has no problem backstabbing his teammates.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:02.480
In fact, Captain Boomerang betrays one of&nbsp;
his colleagues in one of the team's earliest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:08.800
adventures. In 1987's Suicide Squad #1, tired of&nbsp;
listening to Boomer mock the other team members,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:13.280
Mindboggler uses her powers to reveal his&nbsp;
biggest fears. In the following issue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:17.280
Captain Boomerang sees that Mindboggler is&nbsp;
about to be gunned down from behind by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:22.320
villain Rustam. He whips out a boomerang to&nbsp;
intervene but stops himself, remembering how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:27.360
Mindboggler humiliated him. He then escapes&nbsp;
as Rustam shoots and kills Mindboggler.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:33.440
In 1988's Suicide Squad #9, Digger betrays a&nbsp;
teammate by using him as something of a test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:37.920
subject. He isn't sure his superiors mean it&nbsp;
when they threaten to detonate the explosives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:42.160
strapped to the squad members' arms, so he&nbsp;
convinces Slipknot that they're bluffing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:47.520
After a heated battle, Slipknot tries to escape&nbsp;
the squad, and he pays for the mistake dearly.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:52.080
Captain Boomerang's treachery isn't limited to&nbsp;
his early appearances with the squad, either.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:57.520
In 2017's Suicide Squad #13, Captain&nbsp;
Boomerang kills fellow teammate Hack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:01.680
by stabbing her with one of his boomerangs.&nbsp;
So yeah — not the nicest guy in the world.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:07.200
Despite being a pretty unpleasant character in&nbsp;
general, Captain Boomerang is actually behind one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:11.680
of the funniest and longest-running subplots&nbsp;
in the history of the Suicide Squad comics.

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:17.440
In 1988's Suicide Squad #21, Amanda&nbsp;
Waller is walking around Belle Reve prison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:21.920
and considering her recent challenges when&nbsp;
a whipped cream pie flies into her face.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:26.560
Suspecting Boomer is the culprit right away,&nbsp;
Waller summons him — on his way to see her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:31.120
however, a pie gets him, too. It would take&nbsp;
years for the pie-thrower to be revealed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:38.160
The next victim is Nemesis, in Suicide Squad&nbsp;
#23. Then, in 1989's Suicide Squad #26,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:42.960
Vixen gets a pie in the face while getting frisky&nbsp;
with Bronze Tiger, and Bronze Tiger gets one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:49.520
a moment later. Daily Planet reporter Lois Lane&nbsp;
takes a dessert to the nose in Suicide Squad #30,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:54.400
and Jewelee and Doctor Light fall victim to&nbsp;
this creamy bandit in the following issue.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:59.520
The final pie victim is Major&nbsp;
Victory in 1990's Suicide Squad #37.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:04.400
Captain Boomerang tries to frame fellow squad&nbsp;
member Punch by filling his locker with pies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:09.360
but he's found out regardless. It's discovered&nbsp;
that Boomer used his boomerang tech to get himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:14.320
in the face, throwing suspicion off himself early&nbsp;
on in the saga. Boomer then quits the squad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:18.720
laughing in Waller's face that there's nothing&nbsp;
she can do to punish him. So, Waller does what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:23.360
any sensible person would do when they can’t&nbsp;
extract revenge for catching a pie to the face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:26.320
she has him abducted and&nbsp;
stranded on a desert island.

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:32.320
In 2004, DC Comics released the&nbsp;
game-changing miniseries Identity Crisis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:37.360
The story unfolds with the violent death of&nbsp;
Sue Dibny, wife to superhero Ralph Dibny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:42.720
aka the Elongated Man. What follows is a mystery&nbsp;
that explodes into many different threads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:48.480
including a series of shocking reveals involving&nbsp;
misdeeds committed by DC's heroes. One such thread&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:53.280
includes Digger Harkness accepting a high-profile&nbsp;
job that eventually leads to his death.

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:58.560
Identity Crisis finds Captain Boomerang having&nbsp;
fallen on hard times. He can hardly rub two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:02.800
pennies together, and among DC's community&nbsp;
of supervillains, he doesn't command the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:08.320
respect he once did. In Identity Crisis #5,&nbsp;
Boomer embarks on what proves to be his last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:13.760
job — the assassination of Jack Drake. While he&nbsp;
doesn't have any superpowers or fancy gadgets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:18.640
Jack Drake is no standard mark as he's the&nbsp;
father of Tim Drake, the Robin of the time.

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:22.000
Terrified that his sidekick will have&nbsp;
to experience what he suffered when his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:26.000
own parents were killed, Batman rushes&nbsp;
to Jack Drake's home once he learns of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:31.120
the plot — but he's too late. Drake mortally&nbsp;
wounds Boomer with a handgun, but not before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:36.000
one of the villain's boomerangs likewise kills&nbsp;
Drake. Aside from a brief stint as a zombie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:40.880
Captain Boomerang wouldn't return fully to&nbsp;
life until 2010's Brightest Day storyline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:45.440
just before the rebooting of the DC&nbsp;
Universe as part of the New 52 event.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:50.480
After this, Digger Harkness is recruited to join&nbsp;
Amanda Waller's new and improved Suicide Squad.

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:54.560
A couple of years before the first&nbsp;
Suicide Squad film was released,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:59.520
Captain Boomerang made his live-action&nbsp;
debut in season 3 of The CW's Arrow.

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:03.040
"Sorry to do this to you, but&nbsp;
you have something I need."

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:08.480
He shows up in the final moments of "Draw Back&nbsp;
Your Bow" and then gets a lot more screen time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:13.600
in "The Brave and the Bold." In Arrow, Digger is&nbsp;
a former Suicide Squad agent whose last mission&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:18.480
didn't go as planned. Once things went sideways,&nbsp;
Lyla Michaels opted to activate the bombs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:23.680
implanted in all Task Force X recruits' necks, but&nbsp;
apparently, the device in Digger malfunctioned.

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:27.680
"The Brave and the Bold" is one of the earliest&nbsp;
team-ups between Oliver Queen and the Flash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:32.160
and it's clear Team Arrow could use the&nbsp;
back-up. Digger proves to be just as formidable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:36.800
an enemy as his comic book counterpart, though&nbsp;
eventually, Queen and the Flash take him down.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:41.280
Captain Boomerang appears one more time in&nbsp;
season 5, when Queen releases Digger and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:46.400
Deathstroke from their island prisons to help him&nbsp;
defeat Prometheus. Proving just as treacherous as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:51.040
his comic book counterpart, Digger is revealed&nbsp;
to have already made a deal with Prometheus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:55.280
But Harkness doesn't survive to regret his&nbsp;
choice, as he's killed by Malcolm Merlyn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.280 --> 00:09:59.920
who willingly sacrifices himself by detonating&nbsp;
a land mine when Harkness approaches.

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:06.160
Jai Courtney brought Captain Boomerang to life on&nbsp;
the big screen in 2016's Suicide Squad. Courtney's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:10.320
portrayal of Digger Harkness is the source of&nbsp;
much of the humor in the action-packed movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:14.560
and it's clear Courtney and the filmmakers&nbsp;
drew heavily from the source material. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:19.360
there are some significant differences between the&nbsp;
comic book and movie versions of the character.

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:21.920
Like the Captain Boomerang of&nbsp;
the comics, Digger starts his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:26.240
criminal career as a Flash villain. Ezra&nbsp;
Miller cameos as the Scarlet Speedster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:29.600
capturing Digger in a flashback.&nbsp;
And just like the source material,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:33.920
Courtney's version of Digger can't stand&nbsp;
Amanda Waller. The film even adapts Digger's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:38.320
manipulation of Slipknot into unintentionally&nbsp;
killing himself from Suicide Squad #9.

00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:40.640
“Sorry. It's what I do."

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:44.960
There are important differences between Courtney's&nbsp;
Digger and the source material, however.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:49.920
He's much less cruel to his teammates, ultimately&nbsp;
shows himself to be much more of a team player,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:53.680
and in particular, comes off as a lot&nbsp;
tougher than the comic book version.

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:58.960
Jai Courtney subsequently reprised the role of&nbsp;
Captain Boomerang for the 2021 movie The Suicide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:04.160
Squad, making him one of just three members of the&nbsp;
original team to return for the sequel. Whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:09.040
Digger Harkness has a future in the DC Universe&nbsp;
beyond this movie, however, is yet to be seen.

